## 11. ReplicaSet Limitations

ReplicaSets maintain the desired number of Pods, but they are not designed to manage application rollouts.

For example, changing the container image in the ReplicaSet template does not automatically replace all existing Pods with the new version as a Deployment rollout would.

Existing Pods can continue running the old image while newly created Pods use the updated template.

### Solution: Use Deployments

Deployments are recommended for most application workloads because they manage ReplicaSets and provide:

- Rolling updates
- Rollout status
- Revision history
- Rollbacks
- Declarative application updates

Example:

```bash
kubectl create deployment nginx-demo --image=nginx:stable
```

Check:

```bash
kubectl get deployments
kubectl rollout status deployment/nginx-demo
```

Update the image:

```bash
kubectl set image deployment/nginx-demo nginx=nginx:latest
```

View rollout history:

```bash
kubectl rollout history deployment/nginx-demo
```

Roll back to the previous revision:

```bash
kubectl rollout undo deployment/nginx-demo
```

The container name used in `kubectl set image` must match the container name in the Deployment's Pod template.

